>
  <Header />

  <section class="bg-gopro-charcoal text-white py-16 border-b-4 border-gopro-orange">
    <div class="container-custom">
      <nav class="text-sm mb-6 font-sans">
        <a href="/" class="text-gray-400 hover:text-gopro-orange transition-colors">Home</a>
        <span class="mx-2 text-gray-500">/</span>
        <a href="/guides" class="text-gray-400 hover:text-gopro-orange transition-colors">Guides</a>
        <span class="mx-2 text-gray-500">/</span>
        <span class="text-white">Fireplace TV Mounting</span>
      </nav>
      <h1 class="text-4xl md:text-5xl font-display font-black uppercase mb-4 tracking-tight">Fireplace TV Mounting in Orlando & Kissimmee</h1>
      <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 max-w-3xl font-sans">
        Heat, height, wire concealment, and when mounting above the fireplace makes sense (or doesn't).
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section-padding">
    <div class="container-custom">
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto">
        <div class="prose prose-lg max-w-none">
          <p class="text-xl text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-6 font-sans">
            <a href="/services/tv-mounting" class="text-gopro-orange hover:underline">Mounting a TV above a fireplace</a> is popular in <a href="/areas/kissimmee" class="text-gopro-orange hover:underline">Kissimmee</a> and <a href="/areas/orlando" class="text-gopro-orange hover:underline">Orlando</a> homes, especially newer builds with tall stone or tile fireplace surrounds. It looks great when done right, but heat, viewing angle, and wire concealment create challenges. Here's how we do it (and when we recommend a different spot).
          </p>

          <h2 class="text-3xl font-display font-black uppercase mb-6 tracking-tight mt-12">The Heat Problem</h2>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            Most TVs have a maximum operating temperature of 90-95°F. Gas fireplaces push hot air upward, and wood-burning fireplaces radiate heat that can reach 100°F+ directly above the mantel. Extended exposure to heat damages TV electronics, dims the screen, or shortens panel life.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-display font-bold uppercase mb-4 tracking-wide mt-8">Gas Fireplaces</h3>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            Modern gas fireplaces (common in Florida homes) are safer for above-TV mounting than wood-burning. But direct-vent models with top vents still push hot air straight up. If the fireplace runs for 2+ hours, check the wall temperature:
          </p>
          <ul class="list-disc pl-6 mb-6 space-y-2 text-gray-700 font-sans">
            <li><strong>Under 90°F:</strong> Safe for TV mounting</li>
            <li><strong>90-100°F:</strong> Use a pull-down mount to lower TV away from wall when fireplace is on</li>
            <li><strong>Over 100°F:</strong> Don't mount TV above fireplace, or install a heat deflector/mantel shield</li>
          </ul>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-display font-bold uppercase mb-4 tracking-wide mt-8">Electric Fireplaces</h3>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            Electric inserts and media consoles produce minimal heat. Safe for TV mounting. Most <a href="/areas/celebration" class="text-gopro-orange hover:underline">Celebration</a> and newer Central Florida homes have electric or gas, not wood-burning.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-display font-bold uppercase mb-4 tracking-wide mt-8">Wood-Burning Fireplaces</h3>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            Too much heat for a TV directly above. We recommend mounting the TV on an adjacent wall or in a built-in cabinet instead.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gopro-orange/10 border-l-4 border-gopro-orange p-6 my-8">
            <p class="text-gray-700 font-sans mb-0">
              <strong>Florida factor:</strong> Most Central Florida homes don't run fireplaces often (maybe a few nights in December-February). Heat damage is less of a concern here than in colder climates where fireplaces run daily. But if you DO use yours regularly, heat management matters.
            </p>
          </div>

          <h2 class="text-3xl font-display font-black uppercase mb-6 tracking-tight mt-12">The Viewing Height Problem</h2>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            Ideal TV viewing height is eye level when seated—typically 42-50 inches from floor to TV center. Most fireplace mantels sit 50-60 inches high, which puts the TV center 60-75 inches off the floor. That's high.
          </p>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            If you're mounting above a fireplace, expect to tilt your head up 10-20 degrees. For short viewing (background TV during dinner), it's fine. For movie nights or binge-watching, it causes neck strain.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-display font-bold uppercase mb-4 tracking-wide mt-8">Solutions</h3>
          <ul class="list-disc pl-6 mb-6 space-y-2 text-gray-700 font-sans">
            <li><strong>Pull-down mount:</strong> Lowers TV 12-24 inches when in use, raises it back up when fireplace is on or TV is off. Costs $200-$400 + installation.</li>
            <li><strong>Tilting mount:</strong> Angles TV down 10-15 degrees to reduce neck tilt. Standard feature on most mounts we use.</li>
            <li><strong>Lower seating:</strong> Deeper couches or recliners with lower seat height improve viewing angle.</li>
          </ul>

          <h2 class="text-3xl font-display font-black uppercase mb-6 tracking-tight mt-12">Wire Concealment</h2>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            Most fireplaces are brick, stone, or tile over concrete block. Running wires through these materials is harder (and more expensive) than standard <a href="/services/drywall" class="text-gopro-orange hover:underline">drywall</a>. Options:
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-display font-bold uppercase mb-4 tracking-wide mt-8">1. In-Wall Power Kit (Code-Compliant)</h3>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            Best method. We cut two small openings (one behind TV, one near floor) and run a conduit through the wall cavity. Power outlet behind TV stays plugged into a recessed kit, which connects to an outlet near floor level. HDMI and other cables run through the same conduit.
          </p>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            <strong>Works on:</strong> Standard drywall walls adjacent to fireplace, wood-framed walls.<br>
            <strong>Doesn't work on:</strong> Solid brick, stone veneer over concrete block, or tile with no wall cavity.<br>
            <strong>Cost:</strong> Included in most <a href="/guides/tv-mounting-cost-orlando" class="text-gopro-orange hover:underline">TV mounting quotes</a>, or $100-$150 extra if challenging.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-display font-bold uppercase mb-4 tracking-wide mt-8">2. Paintable Cable Raceway</h3>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            Surface-mounted channel that covers wires. We paint it to match the wall. Visible if you look closely, but clean and neat. Best for tile or stone surrounds where in-wall routing is impossible without masonry drilling.
          </p>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            <strong>Cost:</strong> $50-$100
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-display font-bold uppercase mb-4 tracking-wide mt-8">3. Outlet Behind TV (Requires Electrician)</h3>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            If there's no existing outlet near the TV location, an electrician runs a new circuit. Hides power cord completely. HDMI cables to cable box or soundbar still need concealment.
          </p>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            <strong>Cost:</strong> $200-$500 depending on circuit length
          </p>

          <h2 class="text-3xl font-display font-black uppercase mb-6 tracking-tight mt-12">Fireplace Materials & Installation Challenges</h2>
          
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-display font-bold uppercase mb-4 tracking-wide mt-8">Drywall Above Mantel</h3>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            Easiest. Standard TV mount installation. Wires run through wall cavity. This is common in Florida homes with electric or gas inserts.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-display font-bold uppercase mb-4 tracking-wide mt-8">Stone or Tile Veneer Over Drywall</h3>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            We use masonry anchors to mount through tile/stone into studs or blocking behind. Wire concealment depends on whether there's a wall cavity behind the veneer.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-display font-bold uppercase mb-4 tracking-wide mt-8">Solid Brick or Block</h3>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            Hardest. Requires hammer drill, masonry anchors, and surface-mounted wire concealment (cable raceway). No in-wall routing possible. We do these, but they take longer and cost more ($50-$100 extra).
          </p>

          <h2 class="text-3xl font-display font-black uppercase mb-6 tracking-tight mt-12">When NOT to Mount Above the Fireplace</h2>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            Skip fireplace mounting if:
          </p>
          <ul class="list-disc pl-6 mb-6 space-y-2 text-gray-700 font-sans">
            <li><strong>The fireplace runs hot:</strong> Wall temp over 100°F = TV damage risk</li>
            <li><strong>The mantel is 65+ inches high:</strong> TV center ends up 75-85 inches off floor = uncomfortable viewing</li>
            <li><strong>You watch a lot of TV:</strong> High mounting causes neck strain over time</li>
            <li><strong>It's a wood-burning fireplace:</strong> Too much heat and soot</li>
          </ul>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            In these cases, we recommend mounting on an adjacent wall at proper height, or building a custom media console to the side of the fireplace.
          </p>

          <h2 class="text-3xl font-display font-black uppercase mb-6 tracking-tight mt-12">Pull-Down Mounts: Best of Both Worlds</h2>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            Pull-down (or "mantel") mounts let you lower the TV 12-24 inches when watching, then raise it back up for a clean look when off. Solves both the height and heat problems.
          </p>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            <strong>Pros:</strong> Ideal viewing height, moves TV away from heat when fireplace is on, looks great when raised.<br>
            <strong>Cons:</strong> Costs $200-$400 for the mount (plus installation), adds 10-15 minutes to setup, and requires enough clearance above the mantel (6-8 inches minimum).
          </p>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            We install these for clients who love the fireplace aesthetic but watch a lot of TV. Popular in <a href="/areas/celebration" class="text-gopro-orange hover:underline">Celebration</a> and newer Orlando-area homes with tall stone fireplace walls.
          </p>

          <h2 class="text-3xl font-display font-black uppercase mb-6 tracking-tight mt-12">Real Talk: Is Fireplace TV Mounting Worth It?</h2>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            If you rarely use the fireplace, the mantel is under 60 inches high, and you don't binge-watch TV daily, mounting above the fireplace works fine. If the fireplace runs hot, the mantel is high, or you're a heavy TV watcher, mount on a different wall.
          </p>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            We'll walk through your room layout and fireplace type during the free estimate and tell you what makes sense. No upselling, no pressure—just honest advice from 15+ years mounting TVs in Central Florida homes.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-lg p-8 my-8">
            <h3 class="text-2xl font-display font-bold uppercase mb-4 tracking-wide">Need TV Mounting in Orlando or Kissimmee?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 font-sans mb-6">
              Fireplace installs, wire concealment, pull-down mounts. We'll find the best spot for your TV and hide the wires.
            </p>
            <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-4">
              <a href="/estimate" class="btn-primary">
                Get Free Estimate
              </a>
              <a href="tel:4072446873" class="btn-secondary">
                Call (407) 244-6873
              </a>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="mt-12 pt-8 border-t border-gray-200">
            <p class="text-sm text-gray-600 font-sans">
              <a href="/guides" class="text-gopro-orange hover:underline">← Back to All Guides</a>
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

